### What is the Reverse LinkedList problem?

Given the head of a singly linked list, reverse it in place so that the last node becomes the new head and all links point backward. Return the new head. Do it in O(N) time and O(1) extra space. This is LeetCode 206.

For example, reversing `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> null` gives `3 -> 2 -> 1 -> null`.

### How does the three-pointer algorithm work?

The algorithm maintains three pointers: `previous` (starts as null), `current` (starts at head), and a temporary `next` to save the forward link before overwriting it.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class TransactionNode {
    public long amount;
    public String description;
    public TransactionNode next;

    public TransactionNode(long amount, String description) {
        this.amount = amount;
        this.description = description;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.TransactionNode;

public class LinkedListReverseService {

    /**
     * Reverses a transaction chain in place for newest-first display.
     * Used by the National Bank of India statement generation.
     */
    public static TransactionNode reverse(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode previous = null;
        TransactionNode current = head;

        while (current != null) {
            TransactionNode next = current.next; // Save forward link
            current.next = previous;              // Reverse the link
            previous = current;                   // Advance previous
            current = next;                       // Advance current
        }
        return previous; // New head
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionNode t1 = new TransactionNode(10000, "Deposit");
        TransactionNode t2 = new TransactionNode(-5000, "Withdrawal");
        TransactionNode t3 = new TransactionNode(20000, "Transfer");
        t1.next = t2;
        t2.next = t3;

        System.out.println("Before: ");
        printList(t1);

        TransactionNode newHead = reverse(t1);
        System.out.println("After reversal: ");
        printList(newHead);
    }

    static void printList(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode curr = head;
        while (curr != null) {
            System.out.print("Rs." + curr.amount + " (" + curr.description + ")");
            if (curr.next != null) System.out.print(" -> ");
            curr = curr.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

```bash
javac -d out com/bank/model/TransactionNode.java com/bank/service/LinkedListReverseService.java
java -cp out com.bank.service.LinkedListReverseService
```

```text
Before:
Rs.10000 (Deposit) -> Rs.-5000 (Withdrawal) -> Rs.20000 (Transfer)
After reversal:
Rs.20000 (Transfer) -> Rs.-5000 (Withdrawal) -> Rs.10000 (Deposit)
```

### Why is saving next mandatory?

The instant you execute `current.next = previous`, you overwrite the only reference to the rest of the list. Without saving `next` first, the remaining nodes become unreachable and are effectively lost.

```java
// WRONG: loses the rest of the list
current.next = previous; // forward link destroyed!
current = current.next;  // this is now 'previous', not the next node!

// CORRECT: save first
TransactionNode next = current.next; // save forward link
current.next = previous;              // now safe to overwrite
current = next;                       // move to saved next
```

### How does the recursive alternative work?

Recursion reverses the list by first reaching the tail (base case: node is null or node.next is null), then rewiring links on the way back.

```java
public static TransactionNode reverseRecursive(TransactionNode head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) {
        return head; // Base case: empty or single node
    }
    TransactionNode newHead = reverseRecursive(head.next);
    head.next.next = head; // Reverse the link
    head.next = null;       // Remove forward link
    return newHead;
}
```

The recursive version is elegant but uses O(N) stack space, making it unsuitable for very long lists in production.

### How is this the foundation for reversal variants?

The three-pointer skeleton is the basis for all in-place reversal problems. The only differences are where you start, where you stop, and how you re-stitch the boundaries.

| Variant | Start | Stop | Extra work |
|---|---|---|---|
| Full reversal | Head | Null | None |
| Sublist reversal | Position m | Position n | Stitch before/after |
| K-group reversal | Each group start | After K nodes | Stitch groups |
| Rotate list | Varies | Varies | Find new head |

### What are the edge cases?

Several edge cases require attention.

- **Empty list (null)**: return null.
- **Single node**: already reversed, return the node.
- **Two nodes**: one swap operation.
- **Cycle detection**: if the list has a cycle, the algorithm loops forever. Assume no cycles.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ReverseEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Null list
        System.out.println(reverse(null)); // null

        // Single node
        TransactionNode single = new TransactionNode(5000, "Solo");
        TransactionNode result = reverse(single);
        System.out.println(result.amount); // 5000
    }

    static TransactionNode reverse(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode prev = null, curr = head;
        while (curr != null) {
            TransactionNode next = curr.next;
            curr.next = prev; prev = curr; curr = next;
        }
        return prev;
    }
}
```

```text
null
5000
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is **O(N)**: one pass through the list. Space is **O(1)**: three pointer variables, no new nodes. The iterative version is preferred over recursion for production because it avoids O(N) stack depth on long lists.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand these deeper aspects.

- **Iterative vs recursive trade-off**: iterative is O(1) space; recursive is O(N) stack. For production lists that could be millions of nodes, iterative is the only safe choice.
- **Sentinel nodes**: some implementations use a dummy head node to simplify boundary handling, but in-place reversal typically does not need one.
- **Immutable lists**: in functional programming, lists are reversed by building a new list. In-place reversal is a mutable-data-structure operation.
- **Thread safety**: in-place reversal mutates the structure, so it is not safe to call concurrently. For concurrent access, copy-on-write or immutable reversal is needed.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often extend this problem.

- **Reverse a sublist**: reverse nodes between positions m and n.
- **Reverse in k-groups**: reverse every k consecutive nodes.
- **Palindrome linked list**: reverse the second half and compare with the first half.
- **Rotate a list by k positions**: find the new head after rotation.
